472:## 14. Human Review, Guardrails, and Approval Discipline
496:- when agent-generated code, design, or research remains proposal-shaped, keep the human reviewer or approval owner explicit rather than implying autonomous acceptance
497:- keep `review_owner`, `approval_event`, and acceptance state distinct when they materially change what is actually approved
498:- do not collapse `reviewed`, `approved`, `accepted for merge`, and `accepted for release` into one vague success label
500:Do not ask for approval on trivial reversible reads.
501:Do not skip approval on broad or destructive writes.
510:## 15. Verification Doctrine
512:Verification is mandatory before finalize.
520:- completion language matches actual validation state
521:- scope did not drift silently
532:For agentic paths, preserve compact safe trajectory artifacts when relevant:
542:For harness-sensitive or release-sensitive paths, explicitly separate:
549:- `release-gated`
559:When the path depends on tools, long-running work, or repeated evaluation, also verify when relevant:
565:- `approval_event` and `network_policy_event` visibility
569:- reproduction evidence versus post-fix verification evidence when end-to-end task claims are made
573:## 16. Output Contract
580:- `Verification`
584:- high-risk tasks must expand verification
594:- compact control packets such as `Coding-agent invocation pack`, `Goal-monitoring status memo`, `Recovery / escalation checkpoint memo`, `HITL approval packet`, `Resource budget and route-choice memo`, `Prioritization queue / next-action memo`, `Tool capability contract / precondition memo`, `Operational substrate readiness memo`, `Lifecycle event / audit trail memo`, `Evidence target / retrieval-mode memo`, `Source consultation ledger`, `Safe trajectory artifact report`, `Packet compliance report`, `Delegation admission memo`, `Join-quality review memo`, `Release evidence bundle memo`, `Benchmark registry memo`, `Context sufficiency review memo`, `Critique quality review memo`, `Adaptation promotion review memo`, `Route-quality scorecard`, `Coding benchmark scenario memo`, `Orchestration topology decision memo`, or `Memory scope / checkpoint profile memo` when those boundaries materially govern the task
596:- when packet coverage itself is the live review surface, treat `Packet compliance report` as a secondary audit artifact for governance-owned packet-floor doctrine rather than as the first-pass owner of the floor
600:When version comparison, release-readiness, or repeated workflow quality is the live problem:
603:- treat context sufficiency, critique quality, adaptation promotion, route quality, and verification-running discipline as separately reviewable control surfaces
604:- keep packet usage, omission findings, replay coverage, reviewer burden, and rollback signals explicit when they materially affect a release or promotion decision
608:When the task depends on benchmarked or release-grade agentic proof:
611:- distinguish threshold language from a real `adaptation lifecycle state`
614:- keep `release recommendation confidence class` explicit when promotion evidence remains partial
619:- distinguish a named packet from a linked run artifact with `scenario_id`, `run_id`, `cohort_id`, `trace_id`, or `artifact_version`
621:- if the required packet floor for a replay, release, adaptation, or telemetry claim is missing, downgrade the claim before using stronger operational language
622:- if the required packet exists but the recommended companion is absent, keep the weaker companion-missing language explicit rather than silently using the stronger claim
623:- treat `Benchmark cohort manifest`, `Replay runner verdict sheet`, `Adaptation controller audit packet`, `Coding benchmark execution ledger`, `Release promotion decision record`, `Telemetry drift investigation memo`, `Route-switch benchmark verdict`, and `Context substrate scorecard` as the minimum operational floor before benchmark-grade, replay-grade, controller-grade, coding-proof-grade, release-grade, drift-grade, route-quality-grade, or retrieval-substrate-grade language
624:- when a stronger operational artifact exists for the same control problem, follow it and keep the lighter memo as background lookup or mark it superseded
625:- prefer one active artifact per control problem and make superseded lower-strength packets explicit rather than silently emitting both
626:- let a newer compatible artifact supersede a stale predecessor explicitly rather than keeping both active by inertia
627:- if multiple artifacts must be synthesized, keep one explicit join rule for precedence, compatibility, freshness, completeness, and downgrade behavior
628:- reject incompatible merges that would weaken a stronger artifact; preserve split verdicts, upstream source IDs, and `artifact_version` instead
629:- if operational evidence is absent, downgrade the claim before escalating to release, adaptation promotion, or telemetry-trend language
630:- keep failure classes such as `stale context`, `ignored critique`, `no-gain loop`, `rollback`, `false-promotion`, `false-hold`, `drift-triggered review`, `rollback aftermath`, `route-switch failure`, `late clarification`, and `failed fallback timing` independently diagnosable
632:- when benchmark, replay, adaptation, release, and telemetry surfaces interact, keep their linkage reconstructible rather than collapsing them into one confidence statement
664:## 18. Final Rule
668:Preserve correctness, safety, groundedness, bounded change, verification integrity, and honest completion language.
